    <Description>ConstTypeArgs.Bytes extends the ConstTypeArgs.Core library by enabling you to “pass” byte values as generic type parameters, accessible from within static contexts. This lets you customize types in a compile-time–like fashion, effectively mimicking C++ template specialization.

This lightweight solution provides type-level byte values for cleaner, more efficient, and more expressive code, making it ideal for:

- Static Configuration: Define behavior without relying on runtime parameters.
- Type Initializer Injection: Pass values directly to static constructors.
- Flexible Validation: Validate const type arguments in static constructors, field initializers, module initializers, via static analysis, etc.
- Constructors: Eliminate unnecessary instance-level initialization.
- And more.

The package provides const type arguments for 0 - 10, powers of 2, min &amp; max values, and more, plus support for byte arrays and domain-specific types.

Example:

using ConstTypeArgs.Bytes;

// Const type arguments:
public readonly struct _8 : K_Byte&lt;_8&gt;
{ public static byte Value =&gt; 8; }

public readonly struct _32 : K_Byte&lt;_32&gt;
{ public static byte Value =&gt; 32; }

public abstract class InitialValue : Byte&lt;_32&gt; { }

// Usage:
public class Foo&lt;TSize&gt;
    where TSize : K_Byte
{
    public static readonly int[] FooArray = new int[TSize.Value];

    static Foo()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Array size is {FooArray.Length}");
    }
}

// Elsewhere
var foo = new Foo&lt;_8&gt;();  // Outputs "Array size is 8"
foo = new Foo&lt;InitialValue&gt;(); // Outputs "Array size is 32"
</Description>
